WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:18.310
As you walk towards the figure, ask who teaches this. Becke traces a wave of books in the nineteen-seventies on the correct way to wear kimono. One large school opened in 1964 and, Becke notes, runs five academies in Japan's biggest cities.

2
00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:37.110
The first layer goes on. In one Japanese case study, a professional dressing of a furisode used sixteen separate items: padding, under-layers, cords, boards, an obi pillow. Together they weighed about three kilograms, nearly four times a sweater and jeans.

3
00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:45.210
Left over right is old. Becke traces it to a clothing code of the year 718, which followed Chinese fashion.

4
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:55.110
The fold at the waist is easily disturbed. In the yukata study, a bow made kneeling on the floor shifted it most; a standing bow, least.

5
00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:04.410
Women today tie the obi high, from under the bust to the waist. About a century ago, cords and obi sat low, at the hip bones.

6
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:08.890
The knot is tied last, at the back. Walk round and look.
